    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039359": {
      "title": "General Operative, Premises Services",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/10/2026 Job Title : General Operative, Premises Services Pay Scale : General Operative Merged Closing Date : 13-Oct-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie About Trinity: Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2025). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Trinity College Dublin- an introduction Post Summary- A vacancy has arisen for a General Operative to work as part of the Facilities & Services Team with the provisional assignment to the Premises Services section of the Estates & Facilities Department. As part of the Premises Services Team, you will work under the direction of your assigned Area Executive Craftperson (AEC) and in the absence of the AEC you will report to the assigned Premises Manager. Post status: Specific Purpose Contract Hours of Post: 39 hours per week Salary: This appointment will be made on the GENERAL OPERATIVE merged scale (€1,355.20- €1,525.31 per fortnight) at a point in line with current Government pay policy. monthly/weekly payscales. (tcdie) How to apply: In order to assist the selection process, applicants should submit a Curriculum Vitae and a Cover Letter (1x A4 page) that specifically address the following points in their application. Applicants should clearly address their experience and how they obtained their knowledge in their application. Illustrate, through past example, their ability to work on their own initiative and resolve problems. Applicants who do not address the application requirements above in their cover letter will not be considered at the short list stage. Applications must be submitted by the closing date specified through eRecruitment. Any Applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Please see the Job Description below for this position. Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Marion Cox Application queries about this post, please email the Recruitment Partner at recruit@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those whom have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039543": {
      "title": "Head of Finance",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/10/2026 Job Title : Head of Finance Pay Scale : Administrative 1 Closing Date : 16-Oct-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post ID: 039543 Post Summary: The School of Nursing and Midwifery is seeking a highly experienced finance leader in the role of Head of Finance, providing strategic leadership and oversight of the School’s financial operations and development, along with it’s research finance operations. This includes the annual core budgeting and forecasting process, research funding, self-financing and commercial activities, and long term financial planning and financial oversight of strategic and capital projects . As the School undergoes strategic growth and development, the Head of Finance will serve as a key member of the senior leadership team, shaping financial strategy, informing investment decisions, and ensuring the effective management of resources. The Head of Finance will play a key leadership role in delivering the School’s Development Plan and achieving its ambitious financial and strategic objectives. As the School enters a significant new phase of growth and development, strong financial leadership will be critical in supporting sustainable expansion, investment, and long-term success. Post status: Permanent Contract Hours of Post: 35 hours per week Salary: This appointment will be made on the Administrative 1 (Points 1-3), Salary Scale (€71,651 - €92,692 per annum) at a point in line with current Government pay policy. monthly/weekly payscales. (tcd.ie) How to apply: To assist the selection process, applicants should submit a Curriculum Vitae and a Cover Letter (maximum one A4 page) that specifically addresses the following points: Clearly outline relevant experience and how this experience has contributed to their knowledge. Demonstrate, through past examples, their ability to work on their own initiative and to resolve problems. Applicants who do not address these requirements in their cover letter will not be considered at the shortlisting stage. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Please see the Job Description below for this position. Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Frank O'Rourke, School Manager, at E: frank.orourke@tcd.ie Application queries about this post, please email Katie Doran, Recruitment Partner at E: recruit@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039527": {
      "title": "Library Guard",
      "description": "Closing Date: 15/10/2026 Job Title : Library Guard Pay Scale : Library Guard Merged Closing Date : 15-Oct-2026 12:00 Contact Email : Recruit@tcd.ie About Trinity: Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post Summary - The Book of Kells and Old Library receive approximately one million visitors per year. The new Book of Kells Experience opened in January 2024 and includes both the Old Library and a new digital exhibition. The person(s) appointed will be part of a team of Library Guards. They will work in a very busy environment, capable of the physical demands of patrolling the Book of Kells Experience which includes the Old Library building, the digital exhibition space and the providing security for the gift shop and the library’s collections. They also assist in building evacuations and other health and safety matters, when necessary. The person(s) appointed will be part of a team of Library Guards and will work alongside a team of Visitor Experience Officers and student ambassadors. The postholder will report to the Visitor Services Manager and two Library Guard Supervisors. Post status: Specific Purpose Contract The purpose of this employment is to support the Old Library Redevelopment Project. As the role is directly linked to the duration of this project, the contract is not of indefinite duration and will conclude upon the project's completion. Hours of Post: 39 hours per week Salary: This appointment will be made on the Library Guard Merged Salary Scale (€1,368.75 - € 1,540.57 per fortnight) at a point in line with current Government pay policy. monthly/weekly payscales. (tcd.ie) How to apply: In order to assist the selection process, applicants should submit a Curriculum Vitae and a Cover Letter (1x A4 page) that specifically address the following points in their application. Applicants should clearly address their experience and how they obtained their knowledge in their application. Illustrate, through past example, their ability to work on their own initiative and resolve problems. Applicants who do not address the application requirements above in their cover letter will not be considered at the short list stage. Applications must be submitted by the closing date specified through eRecruitment. Any Applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Please see the Job Description below for this position. Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Dearbhla Monaghan Application queries about this post, please email the Recruitment Partner at recruit@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those whom have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039522": {
      "title": "PhD Researcher in Sociology",
      "description": "Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Trinity College Dublin- an introduction Post Summary The candidate will join the PhD programme at the Department of Sociology of Trinity College Dublin, Ireland’s leading university which is located on a historic campus in the heart of Dublin. The Department will provide all logistic, academic and intellectual support and integrate the successful candidate into a vibrant doctoral programme at TCD. PhD students at the department are embedded in the interdisciplinary context of the School of Social Sciences and Philosophy which also includes economics, political science, and philosophy. PhD students in Sociology enjoy a wide range of training opportunities. For example, students participate in a research training programme in the department and have the opportunity to join masters level courses (including courses on research methods). Moreover, our PhD students are supported to attend high-quality international workshops and summer schools. PhD students are expected to collaborate closely with their supervisors and will produce a PhD based on four papers of a publishable standard. PhD students may also have the opportunity to gain teaching experience by providing support within our undergraduate programme (up to 4 hours per week during term time, subject to availability). All teaching-related work is paid separately in addition to the stipend. Application Procedure Informal enquiries are welcome and can be made directly to Associate Professor Camilla Devitt, Department of Sociology at Trinity College Dublin; devittca@tcd.ie To apply, candidates should submit the following via email to Camilla Devitt, Associate Professor (devittca@tcd.ie) in a single pdf : 1. A motivation letter clearly indicating motivation and background (max. 1 page) 2. A full curriculum vitae 3. A concise research statement no longer than 1500 words excluding references (see more below) 4. The names and contact details of two referees (with email addresses included); 5. At least one example of your written work (in English and including some data analysis) that shows your analytical and writing skills in a social science discipline. Applications must indicate “PhD Application in Sociology” in the subject line. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039507": {
      "title": "PhD Studentship",
      "description": "Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Developing a new generation of dynamic socio-marine ecosystem models Marine and coastal socio-ecosystems such as Dublin Bay are highly complex systems subject to accelerated climate change. They are characterised by interacting biological, physical and human processes operating across very different spatial and temporal scales. Climate change will alter not only environmental conditions but also habitat configurations, population dynamics, species interactions, food-web structures and human behaviour. Compoundingly, coastal ecosystems are shaped by strong interactions between benthic and pelagic processes, collectively referred to as bentho–pelagic coupling. These interactions regulate nutrient cycling, carbon transfer, trophic connectivity, and ecosystem productivity, whilst remaining highly 2 sensitive to climate change and anthropogenic pressures. Dublin Bay provides an ideal natural laboratory due to its history of eutrophication, wastewater inputs, urbanisation, and ongoing environmental recovery and management interventions. This PhD will investigate how these processes might be represented in an innovative mathematical manner, without assuming that the ecosystem structure itself remains fixed. Thus, it will delve into the question as to how we might conceptualise and mathematically structure marine socio-ecosystem models if the structure of the system itself can change through time and space. Rather than treating climate change simply as a process that “nudges” an existing ecosystem, we want to investigate modelling frameworks capable of representing emergent, adaptive and potentially reorganising marine socio-ecosystems. The project will explore new mathematical and computational approaches to represent such systems, drawing on ideas from stochastic processes, four-dimensional (space-time) modelling, complex systems, agent-based modelling and artificial intelligence. An important component of the PhD will be determining which combinations of approaches are scientifically useful, rather than applying AI or other computational techniques for their own sake. The project will be supervised by Prof. Karen Wiltshire, and co-supervised by Dr. Erica Terese Krueger (Trinity College Dublin), (Dr.) Michael Mitchell (Trinity College Dublin) and Dr. Sabine Horn (Alfred Wegener Institute). Application Procedure • Applicants should submit a full Curriculum Vitae to include the names and contact details of 2 referees (including email addresses), to: Prof. Karen Wiltshire Karen.Wiltshire@tcd.ie Our Values - putting our words into actionValues matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those who have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039551": {
      "title": "PhD Studentship",
      "description": "Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post Summary : PhD applications are invited to develop advanced neuroimaging biomarkers of Alzheimer’s disease in mid-life, asymptomatic individuals. The PhD student’s role will include designing and performing all relevant analyses to assess (a) the performance of established and novel pre-clinical neuroimaging markers of AD in mid-life in persons at high- relative to low-risk, and (b) the interaction between changes in neuroimaging biomarkers with clinical features, as well as the prediction of clinical decline from the baseline measures. The successful applicant will be part of an interdisciplinary team supervised by Professor Lorina Naci, and based at the Global Brain Health Institute, at the Trinity College Institute of Neuroscience (TCIN), in the heart of Dublin. [S]he will receive top-tier training at the School of Psychology and TCIN, in cutting-edge translational neuroscience tools/methods (3T, 7T MRI) and research, and have international training and networking opportunities, including labs 3 visits in other world-leading universities (Edinburgh, Oxford, Cambridge (UK), UCSF (USA), INSERM (France)], access to unique aging/dementia large datasets, and shared resources. How to apply: Applicants should submit a full Curriculum Vitae to include the names and contact details of 2 referees (including email addresses), to:- Lorina Naci PREVENTDEMENTIA@TCD.IE Please see the Job Description below for this position. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039516": {
      "title": "PhD Studentship",
      "description": "Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post ID: 039516 Post Summary: St. James Hospital and the School of Nursing and Midwifery is offering one part-time funded scholarships for entry into the PhD programme March or September 2027. The scholarships are open to nurses and midwives who are not currently registered for a PhD, but who have a strong desire to discover new practices and knowledge through evidence based approaches. The Joint PhD Scholarship offers a pathway to nurses to conduct research that advances innovation in practice within St James Hospital and associated partners, for example to improve the integration of care, services or health systems. The Scholarships offer opportunities for suitably qualified nurses to pursue a Structured PhD which must align with one of with the nursing strategy pillars in St. James Hospital and of the Trinity Centre for Practice and Healthcare Innovation (TCPHI) research centre in the School of Nursing and Midwifery, Trinity College Dublin offers leading expertise: https://nursingmidwifery.tcd.ie/research/research-themes/index.php Areas of subject focus • Integrated Care • Workforce and People • Digital & Data • Chronic Illness • Cancer care & survivorship • Evidence-based nursing practice, Research & Innovation These are suggested areas, but the list is not exhaustive. Other fields of study that seek to enhance health outcomes for service users of St James Hospital will be considered. Post status: One six year part-time PhD scholarship How to apply: Applications for the part-time Scholarships should be submitted via the following link: https://www.tcd.ie/courses/postgraduate/postgraduate-research/health-sciences/school-ofnursing-and-midwifery/ The following documentation must be attached to the application: • Cover letter and CV • Personal statement including career plan (500 words) • Two letters of reference (academic) and one from clinical area (e.g. Assistant Director of Nursing) • Research proposal of approx. 3,000 words, to include specifically the following elements: - Abstract (300 words, approx.) - Background context and rationale for the research - Aims, objectives and central research questions - Methodology including details of any specialist knowledge/data required - Relevant ethical issues - Milestones and deliverables (Gantt chart) - Where the research fits within the School and College research strategies - Dissemination plans and potential impact The applicant will work with a potential supervisor from TCPHI to develop a robust Research Proposal to be submitted as part of the application process. Trinity College Dublin will charge an application fee. You may receive notification following your application to college that your application to be a PhD student has been accepted. This is a separate process from your application for the Scholarship. You will be notified of the outcome of your Scholarship application separately. Please see the Job Description below for this position. Application and Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Snehal Prabhukeluskar, Assistant Director of Evidence Based Nursing Research & Innovation, E: SPrabhukeluskar@stjames.ie TCD contact: Professor Anne-Marie Brady Director of Trinity Centre for Practice & Healthcare Innovation, E: abrady4@tcd.ie Please include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039536": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/10/2026 Job Title : Post Doctoral Researcher Pay Scale : Post-Doc Researcher IUA 1 Closing Date : 09-Oct-2026 12:00 Contact Email : barrma@tcd.ie About Trinity: Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Trinity’s Current strategic place Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 About the Project: This project is funded by the Health Research Charities Ireland (HRCI) - Health Research Board (HRB) Joint Funding Scheme 2026, co-funded by Breakthrough Cancer Research. The project will seek to establish ferroptosis as a novel prognostic and therapeutic axis in NSCLC. Building on the identification of a previously identified 4-gene ferroptosis signature linked to poor prognosis, it will validate the signature as a biomarker in NSCLC tissues and investigate the therapeutic potential of inducing ferroptosis using a panel of established and novel ferroptosis-inducing drugs. Through integrated molecular, cellular and tissue analyses, including patient-derived lung adenocarcinoma tissues and zebrafish CDX models, the study aims to define ferroptosis-driven vulnerabilities in drug resistant, KRAS - and EGFR -mutant NSCLC and inform the development of ferroptosis-targeted treatments to improve treatment outcomes and survival for patients with lung cancer. Location: Trinity Translational Medicine Institute/Trinity St James’s Cancer Institute, St James’s Hospital, Dublin 8, Trinity College Dublin, the University of Dublin Hours Of Post: 39 hours per week Contract: Specific Purpose Contract, Full Time Salary: This appointment will be made on the Irish University Associations (IUA) Salary Scale for a Post Doctoral Researcher (Level 1) with annual increments in line with Government Pay Policy. Closing Date: Friday, 9th October 2026 at 12:00 noon (Irish Standard time) How to Apply: Applicants should provide: • A Curriculum Vitae • A Cover Letter (maximum one A4 page) outlining qualifications and experience relevant to the job specification • Names and email contacts for two referees Applications will only be accepted by applying online through the Trinity Jobs Portal. Applications must be made by the date and time specified. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Late Applications will not be accepted. Informal and application enquiries about this role should be made to Dr Martin Barr , at barrma@tcd.ie Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging . We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together . We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining . And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those whom have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039524": {
      "title": "Practice Manager College Health Service Trinity College",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Job Title : Practice Manager College Health Service Trinity College Pay Scale : Administrative 2 Closing Date : 22-Oct-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post ID: 039524 Post Summary: The primary purpose of the role of the Practice Manager at College Health is to contribute to the dynamic development of the College Health Service, ensuring that it remains responsive to the evolving medical needs of the students and staff of the College. This involves aligning with and advancing the mission, vision and values articulated in the College’s Strategic Plan. The Practice Manager oversees crucial aspects of the service, enabling its efficient and effective operation, and supports the Director in pursuing strategic priorities. The role’s context is shaped by the College Health Service’s commitment to providing modern, innovative, and inclusive services that make a tangible difference to the Trinity community. Through partnerships, innovative approaches and a commitment to continuous improvement, the College Health Service aims to continue to be a leader in the delivery of clinical care to students, and a flag bearer for College Health services across the Island, and beyond. Post status: Specific Purpose Contract Your employment is for the specific purpose of filling an Administrative 2 vacancy on a temporary basis pending the return of the postholder from maternity leave. Employment is not offered on an indefinite duration basis as the original postholder is expected to return. If the postholder does not return to work the post will be filled through open competition. The Unfair Dismissals Acts, 1977-2015 shall not apply to your dismissal consisting only of the cessation of the said purpose. Hours of Post: 35 hours per week Salary: This appointment will be made on the Administrative 2 Salary Scale (€60,185 - €70,718 per annum) at a point in line with current Government pay policy. monthly/weekly payscales. (tcd.ie) How to apply: To assist the selection process, applicants should submit a Curriculum Vitae and a Cover Letter (maximum one A4 page) that specifically addresses the following points: Clearly outline relevant experience and how this experience has contributed to their knowledge. Demonstrate, through past examples, their ability to work on their own initiative and to resolve problems. Applicants who do not address these requirements in their cover letter will not be considered at the shortlisting stage. Closing Date for Applications: 22nd October 2026 at 12 (noon). Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Please see the Job Description below for this position. Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Hiring Manager, David McGrath, Director Of College Health Service, E: dmcgrat@tcd.ie Application queries about this post, please email the Recruitment Partner, Rachel Jones at recruit@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039520": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post Summary : We seek to appoint an experienced Research Assistant to join the GLOMoL research team in the Department of Sociology, Trinity College Dublin. GLOMoL is a European Research Council (ERC) funded project led by Professor Mark Ward. This programme of research is a country-comparative 'Global study of the macro- and individual-level causes and consequences of loneliness'. The appointment is a specific purpose contract, to commence on 1st December 2026 or shortly thereafter. This role is offered for an initial period of 12 months, with the possibility of extension subject to performance and the ongoing requirements of the project. The successful candidate will have completed a Masters’s degree (MA, MSc, or equivalent) in quantitative sociology, applied research, or related social science discipline. The successful candidate should also have knowledge of or an interest in ageing and the social determinants of health, including loneliness. Post status: Specific Purpose Contract, Full-Time Application Procedure Applicants should submit a Cover Letter, full Curriculum Vitae that includes the names and contact details of 2 referees (including email addresses), to: Name: Ms Lorraine Ivers Email Address: recruit.sociology.sssp@tcd.ie Please see the Job Description below for this position. Enquiries about this post should be made to Prof Mark Ward at wardm8@tcd.ie Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039557": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post ID: 039557 Post Summary: The post holder will be part of a multidisciplinary research team, based at St James’ Hospital in Dublin, working on studies related to interventions (drug and on-drug interventions) for Alzheimer disease, Lewy body and other forms of dementia. Our studies use different methods including imaging, biomarkers and neuropsychological methods to assess healthy volunteers, carers and people with lived experience. The post-holder’s primary job will be coordinating the Lewy Academy programme for training healthcare professionals, producing a policy brief and a systems map of the outputs of EMERALD-Lewy and how they can be integrated into existing policy structures in Ireland. Programme’s goal is to improve the diagnosis, management and lived experience of people with Lewy Body Dementia in Ireland. To this effect, the post holder will work together with the Programme’s stakeholders consisting of clinicians, scientists, industry partners, research assistants, PhD students and people with lived experience of Lewy body dementia and carers to coordinate data collection and drive the work package’s research output. The Research Assistant will be part of Work Package 2 and Work Package 10. The goal of WP 2 is to develop the clinical research in Ireland for Lewy Body Dementia, including the upskilling of current labour force. The leads of the work package are Prof. Iracema Leroi form Trinity College Dublin and Prof. Suzanne Timmons from University College Cork. The goal of WP 10 is to understand the policy entry points and propose a potential model of implementation of EMERALD-Lewy’s research outputs. This package is led by Prof. Kim Nyugen Huong from University of Queensland. The post holder will specifically support WP2 and WP10 by coordinating the running and assessment of the Lewy Academy training programme and developing a policy map and brief for the entry points of the outputs for EMERALD-Lewy into public policy in Ireland. They will also interface with the existing EMERALD-Lewy research team to support other research outputs. Post status: Specific Purpose Contract , Full Time Salary: This appointment will be made on the RA Level 1 Pay Grade according to IUA Salary Scales at a point in line with current Government pay policy. How to apply: Applicants should submit a full Curriculum Vitae to include the names and contact details of 2 referees (including email addresses), to:- Name: Anusha Yasoda-Mohan Email Address: anusha.mohan@tcd.ie Closing Date: Monday 9th November 2026 at 12:00 noon (Irish Standard time) Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039412": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (French Fondation Alzheimer Atlantic Fellow Partnership)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 14/10/2026 Job Title : Research Assistant (French Fondation Alzheimer Atlantic Fellow Partnership) Pay Scale : Research Assistant Closing Date : 14-Oct-2026 12:00 Contact Email : gbhiapply@tcd.ie Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 **Post Summary: **Fondation Alzheimer (FA) and the Global Brain Health Institute (GBHI) partner under the umbrella of the Atlantic Fellows for Equity in Brain Health Program. One of the major challenges in advancing the fight against Alzheimer’s disease and other related disorders is to generate new and bold ideas that have a global impact. With this in mind, Fondation Alzheimer and the Global Brain Health Institute launched the French Alzheimer’s Atlantic Fellows for Equity in Brain Health Program to empower medical researchers engaged in Alzheimer’s research and fully committed to changing the world. The Atlantic Fellows for Equity in Brain Health is a prestigious, interdisciplinary, 12-month residential fellowship based at Trinity College Dublin. The programme develops emerging leaders committed to improving brain health equity and reducing the global impact of dementia, with particular emphasis on underserved and marginalised communities. Fellows engage in an immersive academic and experiential curriculum combining structured learning, mentorship, pilot project development, and engagement with clinical and community settings. Upon completing the programme, fellows join a lifelong global network of Atlantic Fellows working to advance brain health worldwide. Successful candidates can also apply for a Postgraduate Certificate in Equity in Brain Health, awarded upon successful completion of the academic components of the fellowship. Hours of Work: 39 hours per week (full-time) Specific Purpose Contract Location: Trinity College Dublin, College Green, Dublin 2, Ireland Salary: Appointment will be made on the IUA Post-Doctoral Researcher Scale, PD2 Point 3 (€58,578), in line with Government Pay Policy. Closing Date: 12 Noon, Friday, 18th September 2026. Application Process This Research Assistant position is funded through la Fondation Alzheimer. In accordance with the Foundation's eligibility requirements, applicants must hold French citizenship. Application must be submitted through the Trinity College Dublin online recruitment portal: tcd.ie/hr/vacancies. Applicants are required to submit the following: • A full Curriculum Vitae (maximum four pages) detailing qualifications, professional experience, achievements • A Statement of Purpose (provided as a download below on the application page) outlining the applicant's motivation for the fellowship, their commitment to brain health equity, and how they intend to apply learning from the programme in their home community or region. This form must be completed in full and uploaded under the document section of the application. • Contact details of two professional references. Informal enquiries about the programme may be directed to: gbhiapply@tcd.ie. Please note that shortlisted applicants will be required to attend an interview, which will be conducted via Zoom video conference. The University reserves the right to shortlist candidates based on the information provided in the application. The Global Brain Health Institute is deeply committed to building a diverse and inclusive Fellowship community. Applications are particularly encouraged from candidates who are underrepresented in brain health, medicine, or leadership, and from low- and middle-income countries or regions disproportionately affected by dementia and neurological disorders. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039537": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Research Nurse or Allied Health Professional TILDA)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Job Title : Research Assistant (Research Nurse or Allied Health Professional TILDA) Pay Scale : Closing Date : 09-Nov-2026 12:00 Contact Email : shughes8@tcd.ie About Trinity: Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Trinity’s Current strategic place: Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post Summary: The Irish Longitudinal Study on Ageing (TILDA) is seeking Research Assistants from nursing and allied health backgrounds who wish to contribute to a well-established study with a strong reputation for producing high-quality, policy-relevant research on ageing. This role offers health professionals the opportunity to combine their existing clinical skills with hands-on experience in one of Ireland’s largest and longest-running studies of ageing, while developing specialist research skills and retaining substantial face-to-face contact with older adults. Hours Of Post: 39 hours per week Contract: Specific Purpose Contract, Full Time Salary: Appointment will be made on the relevant HSE Staff Nurse pay scale at a point in line with Government Pay Policy [€38,166 to €54,956 per annum], appointment will be made no higher than point 5, €44,213 Closing Date: 9th November 2026 at 12:00 noon (Irish Standard time) How to Apply: Applicants should submit a full Curriculum Vitae (max 3 x A4 pages) and Cover Letter (max 1 x A4 page) to include the names and contact details of 2 referees (including email addresses), to: Name: Sarah Hughes Email Address: shughes8@tcd.ie Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Ms Mary O’Shea, Research Nurse Manager at moshea9@tcd.ie, Ms Judy Sweeney, Research Nurse Manager at jusweene@tcd.ie or Project Manager, Dr Ann Monaghan at ann.monaghan@tcd.ie. Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging . We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together . We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining . And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those whom have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039416": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 These Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions fellowships are co-funded by the AIB Trinity Climate Hub and the EU through the pilot Choose Europe for Science Programme. They fall under the Marine Species Distribution Monitoring project, which will explore how climate change is reshaping the distribution of marine species across the Northeast Atlantic. Applications for four postdoctoral research fellows are sought, in which applicants submit a research proposal. Research proposals may span marine spatial ecology, physiology, climate science, oceanography, engineering, and other interdisciplinary approaches. Research Fellows will pursue their proposed projects under the guidance and supervision of the five co-PIs. Fellows will be based in and across the lab groups of the five co-PIs and their two Schools at Trinity College Dublin (the Connor Lab, O’Connor Lab and Payne Lab within Zoology, School of Natural Sciences and the Basu Lab and Persoons Lab within the School of Engineering). Fellows will also be affiliated members of the AIB Trinity Climate Hub which provides additional supports to climate and nature researchers within Trinity. Research Fellows will have the opportunity to develop independent research ideas while benefiting from mentorship across these complementary research groups. Fellows may also pursue secondments in partner organizations, as appropriate. In addition to the competitive salary, allowances for family leave, mobility and work travel, and research and training costs are included. Applicants should identify one or more preferred supervisors and explain how their proposed research aligns with the relevant research group(s). We welcome applications from people of all ethnicities, genders, backgrounds, identities and experiences. Application Procedure • Applicants should provide the following materials in an email to connorth@tcd.ie with a subject line containing “MSDM application” and preceded by the applicant’s last name (e.g. “Smith MSDM application”) : • A DORA-compliant narrative Curriculum Vitae: suggested template • A maximum 10-page research proposal in the format of MSCA postdoctoral fellowship applications (following part B-1, technical description detailing excellence, impact, and implementation): link . • Horizon Europe Ethics Self-Assessment: link • Details of two referees (including email addresses) • Preferred supervisor(s) from project team Informal inquiries can be directed to soroudim@tcd.ie Full Job Description can be found below Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those who have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039555": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Analytical Chemistry",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post Summary: Our research group is investigating skin allergy related to medical devices. Continuous glucose-monitoring devices and insulin pump systems have become an essential component of the management of diabetes, a chronic disease that affects over 500 million people worldwide. As the prevalence of diabetes increases, the use of these devices is also expanding. These devices are affixed to the patient’s skin using an adhesive patch. Allergic contact dermatitis (ACD) to components of glucose-monitoring sensors and insulin pumps is increasingly problematic as disease management becomes dependent on wearable technologies. The objective of this project is to analyse the chemical composition of diabetic medical devices to understand what allergenic chemicals are present, focusing on emerging skin allergens. We expect to establish the identity and quantity of skin allergens present in devices currently used by patients. In line with best practice, we will take a dual approach and patch-test patients presenting with skin allergy to these devices to identify the clinically relevant allergens. This will allow us to counsel patients on the best strategy for avoiding skin allergy to their medical devices. Identification of skin allergens will also provide valuable information to the manufacturers of these devices to enable replacement of currently used chemicals with better, less toxic alternatives. Salary: Appointment will be made on the IUA researcher salary scale for postdoctoral researcher PD1 at a point in line with Government Pay Policy (€47,273 to €51,176 per annum). Appointment will be made no higher than point 4. How to apply: Applicants should submit a one-page cover letter and a full Curriculum Vitae to include the names and contact details of 2 referees (including email addresses), to: Name: Dr. Niamh O’Boyle Email Address: nioboyle@tcd.ie Please add ‘Application for CDSCHC postdoctoral fellowship’ in the email subject line. Closing Date: 12 noon (GMT) on 6th November 2026 Late applications will not be accepted. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039540": {
      "title": "Research Fellow Post-doctoral Researcher in Advanced Nanofabrication and Membrane Processing",
      "description": "Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Applications are invited for a Post-doctoral Researcher position in advanced nanofabrication and ultrathin membrane processing. The post is funded by an industry partner (Lace Lithography) and will be employed through Trinity College Dublin while being based primarily at Tyndall National Institute, Cork. The successful candidate will join an exciting project working on the development of nanoscale patterning of ultrathin membranes for next-generation atom-beam lithography to enable the production of future quantum, nanoelectronic and nanophotonic devices and aid the expansion of the semiconductor lithography roadmap beyond EUV. The role combines electron-beam lithography, plasma etching, thin-film and membrane processing, and nanoscale metrology. The researcher will have access to advanced cleanroom and characterization infrastructure at Tyndall and will collaborate closely with the TCD research team and the industrial partner. Application Procedure • Applicants should submit a full Curriculum Vitae to include the names and contact details of 2 referees (including email addresses) and a cover letter outlining relevant experience and research interests to: Prof. Richard Hobbs hobbsr@tcd.ie Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those who have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039521": {
      "title": "Research Fellow, C-MAP",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/10/2026 Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 This Post-Doctoral Research Fellow will work with the PI (Prof. Iris Möller) in ensuring delivery of the PEACEPLUS ‘CMAP’ (Coastal Monitoring and Adaptation Planning) project as part of the Trinity Coastal Research Group based in Geography, School of Natural Sciences, Trinity College Dublin. CMAP’s key objective is to promote climate change adaptation and disaster risk prevention and resilience, taking into account eco-system-based approaches. The Research Fellow will co-supervise the project’s Research Assistant, also based in Prof. Möller’s group in Trinity College, to deliver the first and second work package of the research project (‘Coastal Change Monitoring’ and ‘Ecosystem Recovery and Baseline Evidence’). This will involve the placement of monitoring equipment for salt marsh elevation, accretion, sediment composition, tidal inundation and wave exposure assessments. The post holder will also implement additional advanced monitoring, including tidal, wave, and suspended sediment transport processes at all Irish focal sites within the CMAP project. Tasks involve the procurement of appropriate equipment, experimental field design and deployment of equipment as well as data processing, archiving for public repositories, and publication. The activities are thus split between some desk-based and some field-based work. The Research Fellow will assist the project’s Trinity-based Research Assistant to guide project partners in the implementation of sedimentation-elevation table and bed-mounted water pressure based wate level and wave monitoring. Importantly, they will also assist our local authority and non-governmental organisation partners to build a virtual coastal salt marsh hub focused on Dundalk on the east coast of Ireland. This will involve co-creating engagement activities at and around all salt marsh within the project with a particular focus on the salt marshes of Dundalk. Frequent travel to/from project sites throughout the duration of the position is expected. Application Procedure • In order to assist the selection process, candidates should submit in one document: a curriculum vitae (2 x A4 pages maximum), the names and contact details of two referees (with e-mail address), and a cover letter (2 x A4 pages maximum) setting out motivation and suitability for applying for this position. Applications must be emailed by the date and time specified to Professor Iris Möller: moelleri@tcd.ie Closing Date: 12 noon (Irish Standard Time) 9th October 2026. Interviews for this position will take place on Friday 16th October 2026 with the successful candidate starting work on 1st December 2026 or as soon as possible thereafter. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those who have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039542": {
      "title": "Research Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/10/2026 Job Title : Research Manager Pay Scale : Administrative 1 Closing Date : 16-Oct-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post ID: 039542 Post Summary: The School of Nursing and Midwifery is seeking an experienced professional in the role of Research Manager. They will have overall responsibility and authority for the School's research support structures, research support staff, and growing portfolio of affiliated Research Centres. As the School undergoes a period of strategic growth and development, this role will be pivotal, working in partnership with the Director of Research, in leading the implementation of the schools research strategy, directing and managing the broad range of research support activities and ensuring the effective delivery, coordination and continuous enhancement of research operations. The Research Manager will provide strategic leadership across all research support functions, with responsibility for strengthening research support systems, driving the development of research capacity, and ensuring that research support staff and Research Centres operate in alignment with the School's strategic objectives and priorities. The postholder will play a key leadership role in fostering a high-impact, clinically relevant, and internationally recognised research environment across the School and its Research Centres. Post status: Permanent Contract Hours of Post: 35 hours per week Salary: This appointment will be made on the Administrative 1 (Points 1-3), Salary Scale (€71,651 - €92,692 per annum) at a point in line with current Government pay policy. monthly/weekly payscales. (tcd.ie) How to apply: To assist the selection process, applicants should submit a Curriculum Vitae and a Cover Letter (maximum one A4 page) that specifically addresses the following points: Clearly outline relevant experience and how this experience has contributed to their knowledge. Demonstrate, through past examples, their ability to work on their own initiative and to resolve problems. Applicants who do not address these requirements in their cover letter will not be considered at the shortlisting stage. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Please see the Job Description below for this position. Informal enquiries about this post should be made to the School Manager, Frank O’Rourke at frank.orourke@tcd.ie Application queries about this post, please email the Recruitment Partner, Katie Doran at E recruit@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039539": {
      "title": "Teaching Fellow in Anatomy",
      "description": "Closing Date: 14/10/2026 Job Title : Teaching Fellow in Anatomy Pay Scale : Teaching Fellow Merged Closing Date : 14-Oct-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post ID: 039539 Post Summary: The School of Medicine is seeking to appoint a Teaching Fellow in Anatomy. The applicant must hold a degree in Anatomy or an anatomically related science and have a research profile and academic record commensurate to their career stage and the responsibilities of the post. Teaching responsibilities will involve supervision of practical classes, management of dissection theatre activities, lecture delivery and module coordination. The post will involve liaison with, and the support of module leads in the coordination and preparation of anatomy practical class teaching and assessments to students. The successful candidate will be required to undertake teaching related administrative duties and responsibilities as directed by the Head of Discipline. The successful candidate will be expected to take up post on or before the 15th November 2026. Post status: Specific Purpose Contract This post is not offered on a permanent basis as it is designated as a training position. The nature of the role is intended as an opportunity to expand one's research portfolio and expertise in Anatomy, to prepare for a career in the Medicine Industry/Academia. As such, the role is not intended for long-term employment beyond the duration of such training. Hours of Post: Hours of work for academic staff are those as prescribed under Public Service Agreements. For further information please following link below: https://www.tcd.ie/hr/assets/pdf/academic-hours-public-service-agreement.pdf Salary: This appointment will be made on the Teaching Fellow Merged pay scale (€43,859 - €55,779 per annum) at a point in line with current Government pay policy. monthly/weekly payscales. (tcd.ie) How to apply: In order to assist with the selection process, candidates should submit a cover letter together with a full Curriculum Vitae to include the names and contact details of 3 referees (email addresses if possible) and a teaching statement (2 pages summarising teaching experience and philosophy). Applicants should provide a clear account of their teaching experience to date. To include: 1. Examples of their understanding of and involvement in anatomy teaching modalities and how they have contributed to the learning process of others. 2. Their ability to work proactively and examples of problem resolution. 3. Their views on the future of anatomy education at TCD and how they intend to contribute to this. Applicants who do not address these requirements in their application will not be considered at the shortlisting stage. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Please see the Job Description below for this position . Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Dr Denis Barry (Anatomy) at E: debarry@tcd.ie Application queries about this post, please email Katie Doran, Recruitment Partner at E: recruit@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039526": {
      "title": "Visitor Ambassador",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/10/2026 Job Title : Visitor Ambassador Pay Scale : Library Shop Assistant 3 Merged FN Closing Date : 16-Oct-2026 12:00 Contact Email : Recruit@tcd.ie About Trinity: Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post Summary : The Visitor Ambassador welcomes visitors to Trinity, helping them to familiarize themselves with the campus and to make the most out of the attractions we have to offer. This role supports the Visitor Experience team to provide a warm welcome to visitors to the Book of Kells Experience. It also supports the management of tickets, day-to-day operations and ensures other duties specified below are delivered effectively and efficiently, ensuring we are maximizing revenue opportunities. The post holder will be required to work across our tourism services, including the Old Library Building, The Book of Kells Experience, our ticketing kiosks, and supporting visitors in navigating their visitor journey. The Visitor Ambassadors work closely with the Visitor Experience Officers and the Library Guards to ensure a smooth visitor experience is provided. They also work alongside the broader team in the Commercial Revenue Unit, including the Trinity Trails team. There are various shift options, both full time and part time. This role includes weekend and evening work. At busier times of the year, extended opening hours will apply and the Visitor Ambassadors will be required to work both early and late shifts. Post status: Specific Purpose Contract The purpose of this employment is to support the Old Library Redevelopment Project. As the role is directly linked to the duration of this project, the contract is not of indefinite duration and will conclude upon the project's completion. Hours of Post: 35 hours per week Salary: This appointment will be made on the Fortnightly Library Shop Assistant 3 Salary Scale (€1,110.21 - € 1,410.60 per fortnight) at a point in line with current Government pay policy. monthly/weekly payscales. (tcd.ie) How to apply: In order to assist the selection process, applicants should submit a Curriculum Vitae and a Cover Letter (1x A4 page) that specifically address the following points in their application. Applicants should clearly address their experience and how they obtained their knowledge in their application. Illustrate, through past example, their ability to work on their own initiative and resolve problems. Applicants who do not address the application requirements above in their cover letter will not be considered at the short list stage. Applications must be submitted by the closing date specified through eRecruitment. Any Applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Please see the Job Description below for this position. Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Hiring Manager, Dearbhla Monaghan at MONAGHDE@tcd.ie Application queries about this post, please email the Recruitment Partner at recruit@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those whom have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    }
  },
